## Education courses

NICE says everyone newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes should be offered a free **education course**, such as **DESMOND** or **X-PERT**. They help you understand diabetes and how to manage it. Many are available online. Ask your GP surgery.

## Healthy changes

- **eat well**, with plenty of vegetables and fibre, and fewer sugary drinks and refined carbohydrates
- **be active**, aiming for 150 minutes of moderate activity a week
- **lose weight** if you need to. Even a small loss helps
- **stop smoking** and keep alcohol within 14 units a week

## Medicines

| Medicine | What it does | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Metformin** | Helps your body use insulin | Usually the first medicine. Can upset your stomach at first |
| **SGLT2 inhibitors** (such as dapagliflozin, empagliflozin) | Remove extra sugar through your urine | Also protect the heart and kidneys, so often offered if you have heart disease or kidney disease |
| **Gliclazide** | Helps your body make more insulin | Can cause hypos |
| **DPP-4 inhibitors** (such as sitagliptin) | Help your body release more insulin after meals | |
| **GLP-1 medicines** (such as semaglutide) | Help control blood sugar and appetite | Usually injections, and can help with weight loss |
| **Insulin** | Replaces the insulin your body cannot make | Some people need it over time |

Most people start on **one medicine** and add others if needed. Your GP or nurse will review your treatment at least once a year.

## Remission

For some people, losing a significant amount of weight can put type 2 diabetes into **remission**. In England, the **NHS Type 2 Diabetes Path to Remission Programme** offers a low calorie diet using soups and shakes for 12 weeks, with support for a year. It is for some adults diagnosed in the last few years. Ask your GP if you might be eligible.

Some people achieve remission through **weight loss surgery**.

## Hypos

If you take **insulin or gliclazide**, your blood sugar can go too low (a hypo). Signs include shaking, sweating, hunger and confusion. Treat it with **15 to 20g of fast-acting sugar**, such as a glass of fruit juice, and check again after 10 to 15 minutes.

## Looking after your feet

Diabetes can reduce feeling in your feet. **Check your feet every day** for cuts, blisters or colour changes, and see your GP or podiatrist **the same day** if you notice a problem.

Next, read about [living well with type 2 diabetes](/conditions/physical/type-2-diabetes/living-well/).
